rters of new generation capacity is solar,[2] with both millions of rooftop installations and gigawatt-scale photovoltaic power stations continuing to be built. In 2025, solar power generated 9% of global electricity.[3] In 2024, solar generated 7% of global electricity and over 1% of primary energy (2.7% by the substitution method), adding twice as much new electricity as coal.[4][5] Along with onshore wind power, utility-scale solar is the source with the cheapest levelised cost of electricity for new installations in most countries.[6][7] Almost half the solar power installed in 2022 was mounted on rooftops.[8] China is currently the largest producer and installer of solar power capacity; globally, it produces 98% of solar wafers, 92% of solar cells and 85% of solar panels, and accounted for more than 55% of global installed solar capacity in the first half of 2025.[9][10][11] Much more low-carbon power is needed for electrification and to limit climate change.[2] The International Energy Agency said in 2022 that more effort was needed for grid integration and the mitigation of policy, regulation and financing challenges.[12] Nevertheless solar may greatly cut the cost of energy.[4] Solar is important for energy security.[13] Potential[edit] Part of a series onSustainable energy showEnergy conservation Arcology Building insulation Cogeneration Compact fluorescent lamp Eco hotel Eco-cities Ecohouse Ecolabel Efficient energy use Energy audit Energy efficiency implementation Energy recovery Energy recycling Energy saving lamp Energy Star Energy storage Environmental planning Environmental technology Fossil fuel phase-out Glass in green buildings Green building and wood Green building Heat pump List of low-energy building techniques Low-energy house Microgeneration Passive house Passive solar building design Sustainable architecture Sustainable city Sustainable habitat Sustainable refurbishment Thermal energy storage Tropical green building Waste-to-energy Zero heati